---
// Help for the Study Desk preview (STUDY-DESK.md M12, D6; Audit 3, NB3-4 and
// decision 5): one page for the whole desk, the Notebook, /desk, sheets and
// the Greek. It was /notebook/help/ (that address redirects here). A static
// page that reads without the desk: it is noindex, out of the sitemap
// (astro.config.mjs), and (as every generic page) out of Pagefind. The
// Notebook's foot, the first-time choice, the tours and /desk's command row
// link here.
import { MAX_FOLLOWING } from "../../lib/desk-bookmarks.mjs";
import { DESK_STAGE, FEEDBACK_HREF, FEEDBACK_LABEL } from "../../lib/desk-first-use.mjs";
import { MAX_PANES } from "../../lib/desk-layout.mjs";
import { TRASH_DAYS } from "../../lib/desk-records.mjs";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/pages/desk-help.css";
---

<Layout
  title="Study Desk help | Liberation and Inclusion Translation"
  description="Help for the Study Desk preview: the desk page and its panes, sheets, the Notebook, where your notes are kept, saving and opening a copy, and what each part does."
  canonical="https://litbible.net/desk/help/"
  robotsNoindex={true}
>
  <article class="nbhelp" aria-labelledby="nbhelp-title">
    <header class="nbhelp__header">
      <div class="nbhelp__inner">
        <h1 id="nbhelp-title" class="nbhelp__title">Study Desk help</h1>
        <p class="nbhelp__lede">
          The Study Desk is a preview of study tools for reading on a computer. It
          has a notebook that holds notes, bookmarks and highlights that stay with
          you as you read, a page of its own where panes sit side by side, sheets
          you can print or present, and the Greek text. It is switched on
          for a few readers while it is tried out. This page explains it, and you
          can read it whether or not it is on for you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="nbhelp__body">

      <section aria-labelledby="desk-page">
        <h2 id="desk-page">The Study Desk page</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href="/desk/">Study Desk page</a> sets up to {MAX_PANES} panes side
          by side, each a different tool, and each scrolls on its own. Open it with
          the “Open in Study Desk” button in a chapter’s toolbar (“Study Desk”
          where the toolbar is short), the header’s “Study” menu, which also lists
          your sheets, your notebook, and this help, or the “Study Desk” button beside
          “Go to passage” on the <a href="/read/">Read page</a>.
        </p>
        <p>The kinds of pane are:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The LIT</strong> is a chapter of the translation, with the
            verse menu and the selection tools of Chapter View. With more than one,
            the one you last worked in leads.</li>
          <li><strong>My Notes</strong> lists what you have kept.</li>
          <li><strong>This verse</strong> shows everything for one verse.</li>
          <li><strong>Footnotes</strong> lists the chapter’s footnotes, each with a
            way to pin it or add a note.</li>
          <li><strong>Book Intro</strong> is the introduction to the book.</li>
          <li><strong>Greek</strong> is the chapter in Greek, the text the LIT is
            translated from, as in the Notebook.</li>
          <li><strong>Sheets</strong> is where you make and edit sheets (see below).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Each kind has a short description under its name in the menus. “New
          sheet…” under the page’s header starts a sheet at once: it opens a Sheets pane
          (or uses the one you have) and asks which template to begin from.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Search</strong> searches the translation, the glossary, the
            articles, the book introductions, and the Greek. It stands alone and
            keeps nothing about what you searched for. A verse you choose moves the
            leading LIT pane, or opens one if there is none, and a book introduction
            opens in a Book Intro pane.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Following.</strong> My Notes, This verse, Footnotes, Book Intro,
          and Greek follow the place of the leading LIT pane (Book Intro follows its
          book). The lock in a pane’s header, “Stay here,” keeps that pane where it
          is while the others move on; press it again and the pane follows again.
          While it is on, the lock is closed and tinted green, and in a wide pane
          the words “Stay here” sit beside it. A My Notes pane names the chapter
          it is on after its name.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Keys.</strong> With keyboard shortcuts on (“Display options”), the
          left and right arrows, or [ and ], go to the previous or next chapter in
          the leading LIT pane, as the arrows beside its passage do, and / opens a
          Search pane.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The commands.</strong> A row under the page’s header holds “Add
          pane,” “Layout…,” “New sheet…,” “Print…,” and “Help.” “Add pane” asks for a
          column (a full-height pane at the right edge) or a row (a full-width pane
          along the bottom), then for the kind. It is grayed out, with the reason in
          its tooltip, when there are already {MAX_PANES} panes or the window is too
          small to give a new one room.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The title menu.</strong> “Study Desk ▾” in the page’s header opens a
          menu for the rest: “Display options,” “Full screen,” “My Notebook,”
          “Take the tour,” and the feedback link. “Full screen” uses the browser’s
          own full screen, and Escape leaves it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Each pane’s menu.</strong> Select a pane’s name to show something
          else in it, or to use “Add pane” (beside this one: left, right, above, or
          below), “Move” (left, right, up, or down, swapping places with the pane
          next to it), or “Size” (wider, narrower, taller, or shorter). Or drag a pane
          by its header: onto the edge of another to split it, onto its middle to
          swap the two. Drag the line between two panes to resize them. The × in a
          pane’s header closes it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Layout.</strong> “Layout…” offers ready-made arrangements. “Reading”
          is one LIT pane with verse numbers, footnote letters, key terms, and
          highlights turned off; leaving it turns back on only what it turned off.
          The same window sets whether the page opens with your last layout or your
          own default, with “Make this my default” and “Reset to the original
          layout.” Your layout is kept in this browser. An arrangement that does not
          fit the window is grayed out, with the reason.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Print.</strong> “Print…” asks for a chapter, then offers to print
          it plainly, with your notes, or with room to write. Each choice opens the
          chapter in a new tab and prints there.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="sheets">
        <h2 id="sheets">Sheets</h2>
        <p>
          A sheet gathers passages and your own words into a handout for a study, a
          class, or a sermon. <a href="/sheets/?sheet-tour">The Sheets tour</a>
          shows all of this in a minute, on the Sheets page or the Study Desk. Make one in a
          Sheets pane with “New sheet,” or with “New sheet…” on the Sheets page, which starts
          from a template (Bible study, Sermon preparation, Class session, Lectio
          divina, Swedish method, Contextual Bible Study, or Blank canvas), or from one of your own. Add passages with “+ Passage,” by typing a
          reference, or from the LIT pane’s verse menu or a selection with “Add to
          sheet,” or from your notes, highlights, and bookmarks. On a chapter page,
          “Add to sheet” in the verse menu and a selection’s panel lists your
          latest sheets and “New sheet,” and adds the passage without leaving the
          page; the bar at the bottom then offers “Open the sheet.” “Make a sheet
          of these verses” on the search page opens what it made on the Sheets
          page, with the Study Desk offered beside it. My Notebook lists
          your sheets under their own heading, and the Notebook’s Settings tab
          links “My sheets.” “+ Text,”
          “+ Question,” “+ Space to write,” “+ Page break,” and “+ Key terms box” add the
          other parts, and “+ From my notes…” lists your notes, highlights, and
          pinned footnotes on its passages (everything, until it has a passage)
          to bring on as parts: a question note becomes a Question part, any other
          note or a pinned footnote becomes a Text part, and a highlight becomes a
          passage. Point at the space between two parts and an
          “Add” pill appears on a line there. From the keyboard, the pills are
          one stop in the Tab order: tab to one, then use the up and down arrows
          (or Home and End) to move between the gaps. The pill adds a part at that
          spot, and its “Passage…” opens the passage form right under the part
          above, so a long sheet needn’t be scrolled to its end. Drag a part by
          its header, or use Move up and Move down. Every change is saved as you make it, and Ctrl+Z (⌘Z on a Mac)
          takes it back. A sheet open in two tabs stays the same in both: a
          change in one shows in the other. Opening a sheet to edit when another
          tab is already editing it asks first, “Continue here?” If both tabs
          change the same part, the one you are typing in keeps your words and
          says “Changed in another tab,” with “Show that version” and “Keep
          mine.” A passage always shows today’s wording of the LIT, and the
          sheet’s page tells you when the wording has changed since you added it.
          In the list of sheets, on the Sheets page or in a Sheets pane, “Delete”
          moves a sheet to the trash, like a note,
          and “Duplicate” makes a copy of it. The key terms box has “Move before
          the reading” or “Move after the reading,” so each sheet can have the
          group meet the words first or let the passage speak first. “Save as template” keeps a
          sheet’s parts, without its passages, as a template of your own (a sheet
          marked “This is for a commercial project” passes that on). It is
          listed under “My templates,” where New sheet offers it and where you
          can open it to change it or delete it.
        </p>
        <p>
          A sheet started from one of the built-in templates opens with a short
          description above its parts: who the template is for and how a session
          with it moves. It never prints, and it goes away when you close it with
          its × (for that sheet) or once you add, remove, move, or rename a part.
        </p>
        <p>
          A template’s parts start empty: the dimmed words in each say what goes
          there, and they never print, copy, or appear when you present. A
          template’s headings print as they are, as cues, even with nothing typed
          under them; a question, a key terms box, or a Leader only part left
          empty prints nothing, so a question you want lines under needs its
          words (or use a space to write). A passage you add goes under the template’s
          reading, or after the last passage on the sheet. The formatting toolbar
          appears under the part you are writing in, and only there.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Blanks.</strong> In a passage, click a key term under “Fill in the
          blank,” or select words and choose “Blank,” to leave a gap for
          participants to fill in. Each blank can carry a hint beneath it: none,
          the traditional word, or your own. For a key term, the traditional
          word is the usual one for the Greek word behind it, in its dictionary
          form: “traditionally believe” beneath “trusted.” It is not checked
          against any one Bible, so where yours words the verse differently,
          choose “My own hint…” and change the word it starts from. Where the
          verse leaves more than one word possible, the blank has no hint until
          you pick one from its menu. Where the LIT keeps the traditional word
          itself (“faithful,” “demon”), a new blank starts with no hint, since
          the hint would be the answer. A footnote that would give a
          blank away, by using its words or by naming a traditional word the hint
          does not show, is held back until you decide whether it prints. To
          remove a blank, select its words in the passage and choose “Unblank,” or
          press Enter on them with the keyboard, or choose the
          × beside it in the list, which puts the words back.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Print, Present, and Share.</strong> “Print or share” and
          “Present” in a Sheets pane open the sheet’s own page in a new tab
          (the small arrow out of a box after a label means that).
          “Edit” there changes the sheet on that page, and “Close” brings you back;
          “Edit in Study Desk” opens it in a Sheets pane, with the LIT beside
          it, and a Sheets pane’s “Open on its own page” edits it on the Sheets
          page in a new tab. On the sheet’s page you choose a version, “Leader version” or “Participant version.” The
          participant’s leaves out the answers and the parts marked “Leader only.”
          If a blank’s words also print elsewhere on the participant’s version, a
          quiet note above the paper says where. On the leader’s version, “Add
          answer key” ends it with each passage’s blanks and their answers.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Print</strong> prints the version on screen, on Letter or A4
            paper.</li>
          <li><strong>Copy</strong> copies it, with its headings and italics, to
            paste into Word, Google Docs, or Pages.</li>
          <li><strong>Save as</strong> saves it as a file: a Word document (.docx),
            an OpenDocument text file (.odt), or a Rich text file (.rtf). Under
            “Slides” it saves the screens of Present as a PowerPoint file (.pptx) or
            an OpenDocument slides file (.odp). A PDF comes from “PDF…,” which opens
            Print, where you choose “Save as PDF.”</li>
          <li><strong>Present</strong> shows the sheet to a room, full screen, one
            part at a time, with passages you can uncover a blank at a time.
            “Presenter view” opens a second window for you, with the leader’s notes,
            that stays in step with the room’s screen. Press B (or a period) in
            either window to turn the room’s screen to black while you talk, and
            again to bring it back just as it was; an arrow or Space also brings it
            back, without moving on. If you give a part minutes, the presenter view
            counts down the time left in it (and, quietly, the time over), beside
            the sheet’s planned total and how long you have been going.</li>
          <li><strong>Share…</strong> sends the sheet to someone as a link or as a
            sheet file (.md). It starts on the participant’s version; you can
            choose to share everything, for another leader, instead. The person opens it on the Sheets
            page, and “Import into my notebook” keeps a copy.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="making-marks">
        <h2 id="making-marks">Making notes, highlights, and bookmarks</h2>
        <p>
          On a chapter, select a verse number and the menu that opens has “Add a
          note,” “Add bookmark,” and “Highlight” under “My Notebook.” To mark only some
          words, select them instead: the same choices appear beside the
          selection. A note appears in the margin beside its words, a highlight
          tints them, and a bookmark shows beside the verse, in red if it moves as
          you read and in gold if it stays put. In Read View
          you can bookmark and highlight, but notes belong to Chapter View.
        </p>
        <p>
          To take one away, find it in “My Notes” and delete it. Deleting can be
          undone with Ctrl+Z (⌘Z on a Mac), or with the Undo button that appears
          for a few seconds. Ctrl+Y (⇧⌘Z on a Mac) does it again.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="panel">
        <h2 id="panel">The Notebook panel</h2>
        <p>
          The Notebook sits in the margin beside the text. “My Notes” lists what you
          have kept here, “This verse” shows everything for the verse you are on,
          “Greek” opens the Greek text, and “Settings” holds the switches, the print
          buttons, and where your notebook is kept. The » button minimizes the panel
          to a tab at the window’s edge, and that tab brings it back. The “Open in
          Study Desk” button in the toolbar opens the
          <a href="#desk-page">Study Desk page</a>, where panes sit side by side.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Keyboard.</strong> Tabbing from the top of a chapter reaches “Skip to
          content” and then “Skip to the Notebook,” which opens the Notebook and
          moves into it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Hide my marks.</strong> Your marks are your notes, highlights,
          and bookmarks. The key H, or the switch in Settings, hides them from the
          page, so you can read it clean, and puts them back when you press it
          again. Nothing is deleted.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Widths.</strong> On a chapter you can drag the edges between the
          text, your notes’ margin, and the Notebook to make each wider or narrower.
          If dragging is hard, click an edge instead (or press Enter on it): a small
          menu offers “Wider” and “Narrower,” a step at a time. The line between the
          Greek and a word’s entry works the same way. “Reset widths” in Settings
          puts them back. A small window, like Notebook actions, moves to the other
          side of the screen when you click the dots at the left of its title bar.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="footnote-pins">
        <h2 id="footnote-pins">Pinned footnotes</h2>
        <p>
          Beside each footnote is a small thumbtack to pin it (“Pin footnote 3”),
          which is filled and tinted green while the footnote is pinned; pressing it
          again unpins it. In a chapter’s Footnotes list, the Notebook’s “This verse”
          tab, and the Study Desk’s Footnotes pane it carries the word “Pin” too; in
          the popover a footnote letter opens it is the icon alone, and pointing at
          it, or reaching it with Tab, shows its name. In a chapter’s Footnotes list
          the thumbtack sits in the left margin and stays out of sight until you
          point at a footnote or reach it with Tab; a pinned footnote’s thumbtack
          always shows. Read View has no footnotes, so it has no pins.
        </p>
        <p>
          A pinned footnote is kept under “Pinned footnotes” in “My Notes,” with its
          reference and the footnote as it read when you pinned it. If the LIT later
          revises that footnote, the pin says so.
        </p>
        <p>
          On a Chapter View chapter, the thumbtack sits in the left margin beside the
          footnote when there is room. When there isn’t, or one of your margin notes
          is already beside that footnote, it sits on the footnote’s own line.
        </p>
        <p>
          Notes go on a verse or on words you select, not on a footnote.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="print-part">
        <h2 id="print-part">Printing part of a chapter</h2>
        <p>
          In Chapter View, a verse’s menu has a “Print ▸” choice under “My Notebook,”
          and so does the selection panel in Chapter View and Read View. In the verse
          menu it offers “This verse” (“These verses” for a range) and, for the same
          verses, “with my notes” and “with room to write.” For selected words it
          offers “This selection…” and the same two. What prints is only those verses
          or words, headed with the reference, with the footnotes they cite and the
          LIT’s notice. “With my notes” leaves your notes and highlights on those
          verses beside the text; “with room to write” adds a ruled margin. “Hide my
          marks” changes only what is on the screen, so “with my notes” prints them
          either way. The choice appears
          only where the browser keeps a notebook. To print a whole chapter, use
          your browser’s print, or the “Print…” choice on the Study Desk page or in
          the Notebook’s Settings tab.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="ways-to-keep">
        <h2 id="ways-to-keep">Ways to keep your notebook</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>In this browser.</strong> Your notebook is kept in this browser, on
          this computer. Nothing is sent to the LIT Bible team or anywhere else.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What can clear it.</strong> Clearing your browser’s data for this
          site removes the notebook. So can a private window that is closed, a
          browser set to clear data when it quits, and a different browser or
          computer, which each start with an empty notebook. Your browser may also
          clear stored data on its own when it is short of room.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Safari.</strong> Safari can clear a site’s stored data after seven
          days of using Safari without visiting the site. If you use Safari, save a
          copy now and then.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Saving a copy.</strong> A copy is a file on your own computer, and
          it is the safe way to keep your notebook. When you first use the notebook
          you can ask to be reminded: the panel then tells you when it has been a
          week since you saved one. You can change that in the Notebook’s Settings tab.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Other ways.</strong> Other ways to keep it, across your computers
          and the apps, are being planned. Nothing here depends on them.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="copies">
        <h2 id="copies">Saving and opening a copy</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/notebook/#backup">My Notebook</a> can save a copy in three
          kinds of file:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A Markdown file</strong>, to read, print, or move into other writing.</li>
          <li><strong>A Web Annotation file</strong> (JSON), a standard format other annotation tools can read.</li>
          <li><strong>A full backup</strong> (JSON), which holds everything and is the one to open again here.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          “Open a copy” on the same page puts a saved full backup back into the
          notebook.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-everything">
        <h2 id="delete-everything">Deleting in bulk</h2>
        <p>
          Deleting a note, or removing a bookmark or highlight, moves it to the trash, where it
          waits for {TRASH_DAYS} days. Undo (or Ctrl+Z, ⌘Z on a Mac) puts it back right
          away. After that, open the Trash in
          <a href="/notebook/">My Notebook</a> and press Restore, on one item or
          on all of them. After {TRASH_DAYS} days only a record that it was deleted remains,
          so a saved copy is the only way back. To clear this browser, that page
          has “Delete all notes and marks” (every note, highlight, bookmark, pinned footnote,
          and label) and “Delete all sheets” (every sheet, and your own
          templates), each leaving the other alone; “Empty the trash now” is
          there too. Clearing this site’s data in your
          browser also removes it. Save a copy first if you may want it back.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="bookmarks">
        <h2 id="bookmarks">Bookmarks</h2>
        <p>
          A bookmark shows beside a verse: a spot you want to return to. One that
          stays put is gold and stays on its verse until you remove it. In the
          Notebook, “Edit” gives a bookmark a name, such as “Thursday group,” and
          can turn on “Move as I read.” That bookmark is red. Open it from your list
          and read on, and it follows you, so it always shows where the group left
          off; while it follows, its mark is an outline, because it is only resting
          where you are. Looking something up, such as a note in another chapter,
          pauses it where you left off, and a bar offers to move it to where you
          are. Reading on to the next chapter carries it along. A verse can hold one
          bookmark that stays put and one that moves. Up to {MAX_FOLLOWING}
          bookmarks can move this way.
        </p>
        <p>
          “Continue reading” is not a bookmark. It is the one place that is always
          there, at the top of your list: where you stopped last.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="labels">
        <h2 id="labels">Labels</h2>
        <p>
          Labels are your own words for sorting what you keep. Give a note,
          highlight, or bookmark a label, and filter by it in the panel and in
          My Notebook. They belong to you; they are not the topics the site uses
          for its own chapters.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="greek">
        <h2 id="greek">The Greek tab</h2>
        <p>
          On a Chapter View chapter, the Notebook’s Greek tab shows the chapter in the
          Greek of the SBL Greek New Testament (SBLGNT), in its own paragraphs. It
          opens at the verse you are reading, and a “Go to verse” button brings you
          back to it. Like the rest of the notebook, it is for computers.
        </p>
        <p>
          Select a word, or move through the words with the arrow keys, to see its
          details at the foot of the tab. Left and Right step one word, Up and
          Down jump a verse, and Home and End go to the first and last word of the
          chapter. The words are one stop for Tab, so a keyboard reader passes the
          whole text in one press. While a word is open, the line between the Greek
          and its entry can be moved with the Up and Down keys (Shift for larger
          steps), and Enter puts it back. The details show: its dictionary form, its parsing in words,
          its Strong’s number, and an entry from a lexicon. You can choose between the
          UBS Dictionary of New Testament Greek and STEPBible’s brief lexicon,
          which shows the full LSJ entry under its own. Your choice is remembered in this
          browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          The details also list the LIT’s footnote letters for that verse, which open
          “This verse,” and, for a word the glossary covers, how the LIT renders it in
          that verse. A text-critical sign in the Greek opens the apparatus at the
          end of the text, which compares Westcott and Hort, Tregelles, NA28, and
          Robinson–Pierpont. “This verse” also shows the Greek of the verse you are on.
        </p>
        <p>
          To search the Greek, use the site’s search box. Type a Greek word, with or
          without accents, to find every form of it, or a Strong’s number such as
          G4102; the site’s search then shows the Greek alone. To search by a
          transliteration such as pistis, or by an English word, choose “Greek” in
          the search’s type menu. An English word finds the Greek the LIT renders
          that way for the glossary’s terms, such as justness or self-preservation,
          then the Greek words a lexicon glosses that way, such as faith. To see
          where else a word occurs, select its form, its dictionary form, its
          Strong’s number, or its parsing in the word’s details, and the results
          take the place of the text until you choose “Back to the text.” A result
          in another chapter opens that chapter with the Greek tab at the word.
        </p>
        <p>
          A lexicon may give a different sense from the LIT. That is expected. The
          LIT’s footnotes give the reasons for its own choices.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Greek data is shared under the licenses of its sources. They are listed
          under “Sources and licenses” at the foot of the tab.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="search">
        <h2 id="search">Search and commands</h2>
        <p>
          The search box also looks in your notebook and understands a few
          commands. On a page of scripture, for example, type “&gt;tour” and
          press Enter to take the tour again. “Notebook actions,” a link in the
          Notebook’s Settings tab, in the search tray, and beside the field on the
          search page, lists the commands the box understands on the page you are
          on. A row in it runs its command.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="tour">
        <h2 id="tour">The tours</h2>
        <p>
          There are three, and each shows the thing working, opening a menu or a tab
          for you; you can skip a part or the whole tour, none starts by itself, none
          keeps anything, and when one ends the page is as you left it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The notebook tour walks through the notebook on a page of scripture, in
          three parts: marking the text, the Notebook, and finding and keeping.
          <a href="/john-3/?desk-tour">Take the notebook tour</a> on John 3. It needs
          the Study Desk preview to be on for you.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Study Desk tour runs on the Study Desk page, in two parts: what is on
          the desk, then how to change it. It shows the original layout while it
          runs, adds a pane, shows a Sheets pane and the template chooser, and tries
          a layout, and it puts your own layout back when it ends. The first time the
          Study Desk opens it offers the tour, and after that the page’s title menu
          has “Take the tour.” <a href="/desk/?desk-tour">Take the Study Desk tour</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Sheets tour runs on the Study Desk page, in a Sheets pane, and
          on the Sheets page, in two parts: making a sheet (the list, the
          templates, the parts, a passage and its blanks, your own words) and
          using one (printing, sharing, presenting). The sheet it works on is
          the tour’s own and is never saved. The first time a Sheets pane or
          the Sheets page opens it offers the tour, and each page’s list of
          sheets has “Take the Sheets tour.” <a href="/desk/?sheet-tour">Take
          it on the Study Desk</a> or <a href="/sheets/?sheet-tour">on the
          Sheets page</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="sharing">
        <h2 id="sharing">Sharing what you write</h2>
        <p>
          What you write is yours. If you share it with the LIT’s words quoted
          inside, such as in a study guide or a bulletin, the
          <a href="/read/#license-terms">license terms</a> say how the translation
          may be quoted. A sheet’s License card has a box, “This is for a
          commercial project,” that checks the sheet against the commercial
          terms: how much of it is the LIT’s words, whether it quotes a whole
          book, whether every footnote prints, and whether it leaves blanks.
          Once it is ticked, a sheet
          that goes beyond those terms asks before you print, copy, save,
          share, or present it from its page, and you can go ahead anyway.
        </p>
      </section>

